{"packet_id":"d7b0cbc79c49743c","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (whether to invest, and on what evidence) from the suggested solution. It lists alternatives, including expanding through dealers, doing nothing, and targeting a different customer group. It also names the independent-dealer model as a constraint. It does not correct a premise or surface a non-obvious constraint. A1 states that sources are limited to 'before mid-1962', yet A15 later proposes an online panel, so the framing did not carry through to the test."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A11"],"reason":"T1 in A9 names who changes behaviour (small-garden homeowners) and why (low cost, practicality). This is partly grounded in deere-wheelhorse-demand, quoted in A11. The value-capture link is unsupported and internally inconsistent. Selling directly while bypassing dealers rests on no dossier source. A 'premium for brand' price sits awkwardly with a low-cost positioning. 'Growing household garden size and interest in DIY' is asserted without a source."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (utility, low-cost, small-garden owners) and T2 (status, premium, affluent buyers) are real rivals with different customers and price positions. Both assume the same direct-to-consumer channel. No theory considers selling through Deere's existing dealers, which A1 itself flagged."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"The predictions are observable: direct-sale intent compared with dealer intent, regional concentration, and premium intent among affluent households. Their failure would trouble the theories only weakly, because the regional predictions overlap (both include the Northeast). The proposed test never checks T1's channel prediction."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A11","A12","A14"],"reason":"The 'does not show' limits are stated, but source classes are misreported. A11 calls deere-wheelhorse-demand an 'independent_study' when the dossier marks it verified_observation. A12 calls a company_claim an 'independent_study'. A14 calls a verified_observation an 'independent_study'. The trace never addresses date, place, or whether sources fit the customer group. It also ignores the directly relevant deere-green-76 and deere-ctf-1959, which are only listed in search results (A4, A3)."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A13"],"reason":"A13 runs one search against T1, but it does not report what the returned sources showed or say that no contradiction was found. No challenge is made against the direct-to-consumer channel assumption, which is the weakest link in the favoured theory."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A15"],"reason":"A15 is confounded. Group B sees the same standard description plus a premium version, so the arms do not isolate positioning. The scale is described as '7-point (1-10)'. An online panel is anachronistic and infeasible in 1962. The test does not vary channel, so it cannot test T1's direct-to-consumer value capture, which the recommendation depends on. Cost and sample size are assumptions, not results."},"revision":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A17"],"reason":"A17 correctly identifies SYN-D as an irrelevant simulated label-printer result. It retains T1 and does not treat the simulated result as real demand. This is a proportionate, evidence-linked retention. Implications for the recommendation are not traced explicitly, though none were needed."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A16","A18","A15"],"reason":"A18 commits to investing in a direct-to-consumer channel that no source supports. Its reversal conditions contradict A15's decision rule: A18 says 'low-cost version dominates' means no expansion, while A15 says it means proceed with T1. The uncertainty is stated, but the position does not follow coherently from the evidence or the test."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A9","A16","A18"],"reason":"Direct-to-consumer sales bypassing dealers, with premium pricing, is recommended without any supporting source. The dossier only shows dealer distribution (deere-wiki-dealers) and general demand."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A10"],"reason":"A10 states as fact that 'in the 1960s, ownership of a lawn tractor became a visible marker of modernity and success'. This is a hypothesis presented as fact and possibly reflects hindsight."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A11","A12","A14"],"reason":"Source classes are misreported as 'independent_study' when the dossier marks them company_claim or verified_observation, which inflates evidential weight."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A15"],"reason":"Group B includes the Group A content plus a premium version, which confounds the comparison. The test also does not vary channel, so it cannot discriminate T1's value-capture claim. The online survey is infeasible in 1962."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A15","A18"],"reason":"Internal contradiction: the final reversal conditions treat low-cost dominance as grounds for no expansion, while the test's decision rule treats it as support for proceeding with T1."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"The episode is complete but contains critical errors. The direct-to-consumer recommendation is unsupported. Source classes are misreported. The proposed test is confounded and anachronistic. The decision rules contradict each other between A15 and A18. Any one of these is enough to reject it."}
